---
/**
 * MarketShape — "Three numbers you are already held to".
 *
 * WHAT THIS SECTION IS FOR, AND WHY THE CONTENT CHANGED (OA-27, owner decision
 * 2026-08-02). It used to lead with market statistics: 40,000+ notices a year,
 * 55% stopped at qualification, 44% citing process complexity. Three of those
 * four could not be sourced. Worse than misremembered: CIPS and techUK have
 * published on exactly that subject and neither 55% nor 44% appears anywhere in
 * their data, and 40,000+ was traceable only to commercial tender-alert
 * marketing copy. On a site whose whole argument is that every figure traces to
 * something, that was the worst place on the page to be loose.
 *
 * The replacement is not a better-sourced market statistic. It is a different
 * kind of number: figures the reader is already held to, each resting on statute
 * or a Cabinet Office notice.
 *
 * ── WHY THE SOURCE NOTE IS GONE, AND WHERE IT WENT ──────────────────────────
 *
 * Owner review, 2026-08-02, second pass: "For 'Duties, not estimates' why do we
 * have so much text? If you want to give sources, give one link to a page where
 * we cite all the sources. Why complicate the home page?" Followed by the
 * sharper verdict on the page as a whole: it reads as a research site rather
 * than a product site.
 *
 * That is right, and it was this section's 90-word source note more than
 * anything else. The note is not deleted. It moved to /sources, in FULLER form
 * than it had here: every figure now has a row in a table on that page, each
 * instrument has its dates, its threshold and a link to legislation.gov.uk or
 * gov.uk, and the two Procurement Act duties are two separate records. The
 * accuracy was relocated, not relaxed, and the page it went to is the more
 * rigorous of the two.
 *
 * ONE LINK, ONCE. The homepage links /sources from here and from
 * ReasoningTrace, and from nowhere else. Scattering the citations back into the
 * sections, one "source:" clause at a time, is exactly how the note grew the
 * first time.
 *
 * ── THE STAGE LABEL BECAME THE CITATION TAG ─────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Each stop used to carry a stage word (Evaluation, Threshold, At signature,
 * Every year after) in the mono slot above the numeral. That slot now holds the
 * instrument instead: s.52, s.71, PPN 002. Same treatment, same position, same
 * accent colour, one fewer thing to read.
 *
 * A MONO TAG NEXT TO A NUMERAL IS A LABEL, NOT A CITATION. It names the
 * instrument in four characters and points at the page that actually cites it.
 * That is the line this section may not cross: the moment a tag grows a date, a
 * threshold or the words "in force", the source note is being rebuilt in place.
 *
 * ── THE SET IS "SET C", THREE FIGURES, ONE PER INSTRUMENT (owner decision, ───
 *    2026-08-05)
 *
 * It was four: 10%, £5m, 3 and 12. The rule the owner's set applies is ONE
 * FIGURE PER INSTRUMENT WITH NO SECTION REPEATED, and the old set broke it by
 * carrying s.52 twice. `3` (KPIs published before signing, s.52) is the figure
 * that went, because between the two s.52 stops it was the weaker: measured at
 * 1440 it filled 14% of its column against 55% and 54% for its neighbours, so
 * the row's width ratio ran 3.93:1. Set C runs 1.91:1, and that ratio — not the
 * figure's accuracy — is what the owner was objecting to.
 *
 * 10% IS DEMOTED IN POSITION, NOT CHANGED IN VALUE, AND THIS IS THE ONE LINE
 * HERE THAT IS AN ACCURACY RULE RATHER THAN A DESIGN ONE. The owner's words were
 * "10% is very overused", which is an objection to it LEADING the section. It is
 * correct and mandatory under PPN 002, CLAUDE.md rule 8 forbids restating it as
 * anything else, and check-facts.js fails the build on 5%. Moving it to third is
 * the whole of what was asked for. Nothing may "improve" the number itself.
 *
 * THE HONEST COST, RESTATED BECAUSE THE OLD ONE STOPPED BEING TRUE. The four
 * stops used to be ordered IN TIME — evaluation, threshold, signature, every
 * year after — and this note used to claim the reading order still carried that
 * sequence. Set C does not, and pretending otherwise would be exactly the kind
 * of comment directive W8 exists to stop: 10% bites at EVALUATION, which is the
 * earliest of the three, and it now reads last. The order is instrument order —
 * Procurement Act s.52, then s.71, then the Cabinet Office note — which is also
 * the order they appear in on /sources, so the two pages can be read against
 * each other. The accent ramp still runs teal to cyan to violet across the three
 * and still says the same weaker thing it always said: that this is one set read
 * in one direction. It no longer claims that direction is time.
 *
 * DO NOT MERGE SECTION 52 AND SECTION 71. s.52 sets and publishes the KPIs.
 * s.71 assesses performance against them and publishes the result. Collapsing
 * the two was a live accuracy defect on this site (OA-26). One stop carries s.52
 * and one carries s.71, and the tags are what keep them apart at a glance.
 *
 * WHY IT IS A SEQUENCE AND NOT A STAT GRID. Three numbers in three equal cards
 * with a coloured accent each is the default answer, and it throws away the one
 * thing this section actually knows: the figures are a set, read in an order.
 * The coloured rule above each figure is one continuous line across the three
 * stops. What that rule may NOT do is get shorter or longer per figure — see the
 * refusal recorded at `.spine__stop` below, which is the load-bearing half of
 * the 2026-08-05 layout decision.
 *
 * NO COUNT-UP ANIMATION. The legacy version animated these from zero on scroll,
 * which is the hide-then-reveal pattern that left 4 of 9 homepage sections
 * permanently invisible when the observer did not fire. A number that might not
 * arrive is worse than a number that does not animate.
 */
import Section from '../layout/Section.astro';

interface Stop {
  /**
   * The instrument, in the form it is printed on the page. Four characters or
   * so. It is a LABEL: it names what the figure comes from and sends the reader
   * to /sources for the citation itself. It may never grow into one.
   */
  tag: string;
  value: string;
  /** A few words. No full stop, no date, no threshold, no "in force". */
  label: string;
  accent: string;
}

/*
 * SET C. Three figures, in the owner's order, 2026-08-05. See the header note
 * for why `3` went and why 10% is last rather than first.
 *
 * THE RAMP SPENDS THREE OF ITS FOUR ACCENTS AND STOPS AT VIOLET. It ran
 * start -> mid -> end -> far (teal, cyan, violet, orchid) across four stops;
 * with three it runs start -> mid -> end, which keeps the ramp contiguous and
 * evenly spent rather than skipping a rung to keep an endpoint. --c-ambient-far
 * is simply unused here now. It is still declared in tokens.css and still used
 * elsewhere, so nothing is orphaned by this.
 *
 * EVERY FIGURE HERE HAS A ROW IN HOMEPAGE_MAP IN src/pages/sources.astro. That
 * is a hard pairing, not a convention: a numeral on this page with no row there
 * is a defect on both pages, and the `3` row was deleted in the same edit that
 * deleted this stop.
 */
const STOPS: Stop[] = [
  {
    tag: 's.52',
    value: '£5m',
    label: 'contract value threshold',
    accent: 'var(--c-ambient-start)',
  },
  {
    tag: 's.71',
    value: '12',
    label: 'months between published assessments',
    accent: 'var(--c-ambient-mid)',
  },
  {
    tag: 'PPN 002',
    value: '10%',
    label: 'minimum social value weighting',
    accent: 'var(--c-ambient-end)',
  },
];
---

{/*
  ── THIS SECTION IS BASE, NOT FLOOR, FROM 2026-08-04 ─────────────────────────

  It carried `class="sv-floor"` and the note here read "This is the first floor
  on the page; the hero directly above it is base." Both halves stopped being
  true when the homepage was rebuilt to the owner's nine-section order: this is
  now section 5, and the section directly above it (HowItRuns) is floor.

  specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §3 asks that sections ALTERNATE floor and base down
  the page, so that a section is one or the other and never ambiguous and a
  raised panel always has something to be raised above. Two floors in a row is
  the one arrangement that breaks it. The page now runs base, floor, base,
  floor, base, floor, base, floor, and this is the fifth of those: base.

  Nothing else about the section moved in THAT change. `sv-floor` only sets a
  background colour; the spine, the stops, the ambient rig and the raised panel
  were untouched by it. (The stops themselves changed on 2026-08-05, four to
  three, for reasons that have nothing to do with the base/floor alternation.)

  ── AND THE EYEBROW NOW NAMES WHAT THE FIGURES ARE ──────────────────────────

  "Duties, not estimates" -> "Statutory figures", owner instruction 2026-08-04,
  which names this section in the agreed order. The heading did NOT move with
  it, and that is deliberate rather than an omission. The concept heads the same
  numbers "The shape of a UK bid, in four numbers", which is the legacy
  homepage's own heading and is recorded in check-content-parity.js as a
  deliberate loss for two reasons that both still hold: it led with figures that
  could not be sourced (OA-27), and it caps the section at UK. "Three numbers
  you are already held to" says the same thing about the same figures without
  scoping the product, which is the distinction OA-33 turns on. Restoring the
  old wording would reverse both decisions to gain nothing.

  THE COUNT WORD IN THE HEADING IS NOT DECORATION. It read "Four numbers" until
  2026-08-05 and the set became three that day. A heading that counts the things
  under it has to be re-read whenever STOPS changes length; the exemption reason
  in check-content-parity.js quotes this heading and was corrected in the same
  edit, for the same reason.
*/}
<Section eyebrow="Statutory figures" title="Three numbers you are already held to">
  {/*
    `data-lit` is the whole motion contract for this section, and the reason it
    is safe here specifically. The rule above the stops is drawn at full track
    from first paint and every figure is authored text. Nothing below is waiting
    to be revealed, so if src/scripts/motion.ts never loads, never runs, or
    throws, this attribute stays unset, no keyframe rule matches, and the
    section is complete. See the header note: that is the ONLY basis on which
    this particular section is allowed to animate at all.
  */}
  <div class="ms" data-spot="off" data-lit data-lit-threshold="0.35">
    {/*
      Ambient, per the lighting rig (Figma `141:2` L03), CUT TO ONE FIELD on
      2026-08-05. It was two, teal high-left and orchid low-right; it is now the
      high-left field alone, in violet, screen-blended so it adds light rather
      than painting grey over the page. The reason is beside `.ms__amb-a` below.
      The vignette on ::after is in the PAGE colour and never black, so it is
      not a hue and it stays.
    */}
    <div class="ms__amb" data-field aria-hidden="true">
      <b class="ms__amb-a"></b>
      {/*
        The cursor light and the grain, both mounted INSIDE the ambient wrapper.
        That wrapper is z-index -1 in an isolated stacking context, so neither
        can ever reach the surface a word sits on, and both inherit its radial
        mask so neither can draw its own rectangle at the section edges. <i>
        rather than <b>, so they join neither the drift nor its phase offsets.
      */}
      <i class="sv-spot"></i>
      <i class="sv-grain"></i>
    </div>

    {/*
      THE PANEL IS A <figure> AND THE STOPS ARE STILL AN <ol>.

      A figure, because this is a drawing: one continuous rule with three stops
      hung off it, each stop's tag and label positioned against its own segment
      rather than against the panel. A rule that asked those labels to sit on
      the panel's centre line would take each of them off the segment it names,
      which is why scripts/check-render.js excludes <figure> and why this is
      marked as one rather than added to that gate's allow-list.

      An <ol>, because the three stops are a set read in an order and the list
      is what carries it. A <figure> cannot hold list semantics, so the panel wraps the
      list instead of replacing it.

      IT ALSO FIXES INVALID MARKUP. The travelling-light <span> below was a
      DIRECT CHILD OF THE <ol>, which the HTML content model does not allow (an
      <ol> takes <li>, <script> and <template> and nothing else). It now sits in
      the figure, where it is both valid and still positioned against the
      panel — `.surface` is `position: relative`, so the containing block it
      measures against is unchanged.

      ── IT IS `.surface surface--panel` NOW, NOT `sv-raised` (A-104 item 6) ───

      Owner, 2026-08-05: "Statutory figures aligned with the other cards on
      spacing and theme." The two recipes were never far apart — both are opaque
      blocks at --radius-card carrying a hairline — and on 2026-08-05 they
      stopped agreeing on the one thing a reader actually sees:

        shadow  `.surface--panel` casts NOTHING, by owner decision that day, on
                the fidelity finding that the concept's `.showcase` and
                `.pipeline` declare no box-shadow at all. `.sv-raised` still
                carries --gloss-inset plus the five-layer --shadow-ladder. So
                this panel was the one raised block on the lower page still
                casting a 60px pool under itself while ProofGate, ProductScreens
                and HowItRuns cast none.
        fill    `.surface--panel` is a vertical ramp from --c-panel into
                --c-bg/--c-floor, which is what makes a panel look SEATED in the
                page rather than pasted onto it. `.sv-raised` is a flat
                --c-raised under a 5% wash, which ends on a hard edge at its
                foot.

      Nothing about the drawing moved. `.surface` supplies `position: relative`
      exactly as `.sv-raised` did, so .spine__track and .spine__stop::before
      measure against the same containing block, and both recipes resolve
      --radius-card, so the corner is the same 16px it was.

      THE ONE THING `.surface` DOES NOT CARRY IS THE <figure> MARGIN RESET.
      styles/surfaces.css zeroes it for `figure.sv-raised` and not for `.surface`,
      so it is zeroed in this file's own rule below. That is the single line
      whose removal would silently put 40px of user-agent inset back on the
      drawing.
    */}
    <figure class="surface surface--panel spine__panel">
      {/*
        THE TRAVELLING HIGHLIGHT, AND IT IS AN OVERLAY ON A COMPLETE LINE
        RATHER THAN A WIPE OF AN INCOMPLETE ONE. The distinction is the whole
        reason this passes: a wipe needs the rule to start missing, and a rule
        that starts missing is a rule that stays missing when the trigger does
        not fire. This sits ON TOP of the finished spine and adds light to it.
        Sized and positioned entirely in CSS; it holds nothing and says nothing.
      */}
      <span class="spine__track" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="spine__light"></span>
      </span>

      <ol class="spine">
      {
        STOPS.map((s) => (
          <li class="spine__stop" style={`--accent: ${s.accent}`}>
            <span class="meta spine__tag">{s.tag}</span>
            {/*
              THE FIGURE IS THE CONTENT HERE, WHICH IS WHY IT TAKES THE NUMERAL
              RECIPE AND THE HEADING ABOVE IT DOES NOT MOVE. specs/
              ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.3 measured these at 57.6px against a
              46.4px heading — a ratio of 1.24, which is no contrast at all in
              the one place on the page where the number outranks the words
              around it. `sv-numeral` is the Better Stack recipe from the craft
              table, defined once in styles/surfaces.css with the solid
              full-contrast fallback FIRST. THE SIZE IS WHATEVER --t-numeral
              SAYS AND IS NOT RESTATED HERE: this note used to claim 108px, and
              the token has since come down to clamp(2.75rem, 5.6vw, 5rem) — an
              80px ceiling — on the owner's finding that the figures read too
              big. A comment naming a pixel value it does not set is how that
              went stale, so it names the token instead. tokens.css carries the
              reasoning against the 128px the gap document proposes.
            */}
            <span class="spine__value sv-numeral">{s.value}</span>
            <span class="spine__label">{s.label}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
      </ol>
    </figure>

    <p class="spine__more">
      <a href="/sources#homepage">Every figure, sourced</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .ms {
    position: relative;
    /* Contains the ambient fields' stacking so a screen-blended blob can never
       climb over the copy, and stops the blur widening the page. */
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /*
   * THE HORIZONTAL BLEED IS EXACTLY ONE GUTTER, AND THAT NUMBER IS NOT A
   * GUESS. An ambient field has to reach the viewport edge or it draws a
   * visible vertical seam one gutter in from it. It must not reach one pixel
   * further, because the sitewide gate fails any page whose scrollWidth exceeds
   * its clientWidth at 390, 768 or 1440.
   *
   * This element's containing block is the section body, which is the measure
   * inset by --gutter. Bleeding by exactly --gutter therefore lands on the
   * viewport edge at every width below the measure and stays inside it above.
   * A percentage cannot do this: -6% of the content box is 21px at 390 and
   * 41px at 768, neither of which is the gutter, and both of which overflowed.
   */
  .ms__amb {
    position: absolute;
    /* The scroll-driven parallax throw for this rig. Smaller than the hero's
       48px, and deliberately: seven fields all travelling at one speed is one
       sheet moving, which is not parallax. See styles/tokens.css. */
    --par: 26px;
    inset-block: -20%;
    inset-inline: calc(-1 * var(--gutter));
    z-index: -1;
    pointer-events: none;
    overflow: hidden;
    /*
     * ALL FOUR EDGES MUST BE MASKED, AND THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL POLISH.
     *
     * `overflow: hidden` is what stops the bleed widening the page, but it cuts
     * a 140px blur off square, and a blurred field ending in a straight line
     * draws a visible lighter RECTANGLE on the page. Measured twice on the
     * built result: first as a hard horizontal band, then, after masking only
     * the block axis, as two faint vertical edges at 1440.
     *
     * THE SECOND ONE IS THE INSTRUCTIVE MISTAKE. The first fix reasoned that
     * the inline edges sit on the viewport edge and need no mask. That is true
     * only BELOW the measure. At 1440 the container is 1256px centred in a
     * 1440px viewport, so both inline cuts land mid-page and both showed.
     *
     * One radial mask fades every edge at once and cannot be got wrong that
     * way: there is no axis for it to miss. It also needs no mask-composite,
     * which is the one part of masking whose syntax still differs between
     * WebKit and everything else.
     */
    /*
     * `closest-side`, NOT `115% 105%`, AND THAT IS A MEASURED CORRECTION.
     *
     * The earlier value was chosen to fade all four edges at once, which it
     * did — but only partly. An ellipse of 115% x 105% has semi-axes of 0.575w
     * and 0.525h, so the middle of the left edge sits at r = 0.5 / 0.575 =
     * 0.87 of the way along a ramp that only reaches transparent at 1.0. About
     * 25% of the field survives there. On a 150px blur whose own falloff is
     * nearly finished, 25% of nearly nothing is nothing, which is why it held.
     *
     * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §3 raises the orb stops by roughly 1.5x to
     * 1.9x, and 25% of that is a visible vertical line. Measured on the built
     * result at 1440: a lighter RECTANGLE across this section and the four
     * others sharing this recipe, one gutter in from each side — exactly the
     * defect the original note describes and thought it had closed.
     *
     * `closest-side` gives semi-axes of 0.5w and 0.5h, so the ramp reaches
     * transparent EXACTLY at the middle of every edge and past it at every
     * corner. There is no arithmetic left to get wrong, and it stays correct
     * when a section changes shape.
     */
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(
      closest-side,
      rgb(0 0 0) 42%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    mask-image: radial-gradient(closest-side, rgb(0 0 0) 42%, transparent 100%);
  }

  .ms__amb b {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    /* The board specifies 54-58px at the frame's own scale and 140px at full
       width. This band is the full measure, so it takes the wide value. */
    filter: blur(140px);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  /* ONE FIELD, ONE HUE, AND IT IS VIOLET. It was teal high-left and orchid
     low-right, which is two hues in a section whose whole subject is an accent
     ramp across its stops, one accent each, and the light behind them was
     quietly spending two of those accents on itself. The owner's rule of
     2026-08-05 allows a hue change once per section, on the element the section
     is about, and here that element is the ramp. So the ramp keeps every hue it
     had and the FIELD gives its up.

     THE RAMP WAS FOUR STOPS WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN AND IS THREE NOW — teal, cyan
     and violet — which changes nothing about the argument: violet is still on
     the row, so the field is still spending a hue the ramp already owns rather
     than introducing a fifth. Orchid has left the section entirely.

     THE ORCHID FIELD IS DELETED RATHER THAN RECOLOURED. Its own note argued
     that a field names nothing and that the refusal mark three sections down
     stays unmistakable, which was true when it was the only orchid on this
     screen. It is a weaker argument once the section directly above it is
     drawing a market in orchid, and it costs nothing to settle: one field is
     what the concept paints and one field is what A-103 asked for.

     Violet, not the teal it replaces, for the reason recorded in
     HeroStack.astro: teal is VERIFIED and orchid is REFUSED, so violet is the
     only hue on the site free to be light. Geometry and opacity are exactly
     what the teal field carried, so the section's exposure and its lighting
     direction are unchanged. */
  .ms__amb-a {
    width: 520px;
    height: 380px;
    left: -4%;
    top: -30%;
    background: var(--orb-violet);
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  .ms__amb::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    /* --grad-vignette. Size and centre are this field's geometry; the falloff
       and strength were 46%/82% here against 42%/88% on the hero, and are now
       the token's 44%/85% in both. */
    --vig: 760px 520px at 50% 50%;
    background: var(--grad-vignette);
  }

  /*
   * THE SPINE STANDS ON A PANEL, AND THE PANEL IS REACHED THROUGH THE SHARED
   * CLASSES RATHER THAN RESTATED HERE.
   *
   * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.4: all seven sections sat on one background
   * plus one 55%-alpha card, against 22 stepped surfaces on the closest dark
   * comparator. The fix is a ladder, not a lighter page — our median background
   * luminance and Better Stack's are the same 0.0055 to three decimal places.
   *
   * `.surface surface--panel` is defined once in styles/surfaces.css and every
   * ingredient of it — border, radius, fill, backdrop, shadow, hover — arrives
   * from there. NOT ONE OF THEM IS RESTATED IN THIS FILE, which is the rule that
   * file exists to hold: an Astro scoped style is unlayered and would win, so a
   * restated `background` here would silently cancel the panel ramp and a
   * restated `border-radius` would put a 21st card recipe back on the site.
   * See the note beside the <figure> above for what moved and why.
   *
   * PADDING RATHER THAN A FLUSH EDGE. The accent rules are `border-top` on the
   * stops, so with no padding they would land in the same pixel as the panel's
   * own hairline and the two would fight. The block padding puts the rail
   * clearly inside the panel, which is also what Figma `214:92` draws.
   *
   * --blk-pad-md, NOT --blk-pad, AND THE RUNG IS CHOSEN BY WHAT THE BOX HOLDS.
   * tokens.css names --blk-pad-md "a panel that holds cards in a ROW: the
   * pipeline", and that is exactly this box: three stops side by side on one
   * rail, the same shape HowItRuns' pipeline panel takes at the same rung. It
   * is a 2px move at 1440 (34 -> 36) and none at 390, where both clamp to 20-22,
   * so this is the section joining a ladder rather than being resized.
   *
   * INLINE PADDING STAYS 0, DELIBERATELY, AND IT IS NOT AN OMISSION. Each stop
   * draws its own third of the rail as a full-width border-top, so the rail has
   * to reach the panel's own inline edges. `surface--pad` would put --blk-pad
   * on all four sides and end the rail one pad short of each edge. Same reason
   * BothSides' rail panel takes block padding only. The inset the CONTENT needs
   * from the panel edge is supplied one level down, by `.spine__stop`'s own
   * inline padding, which is uniform across the stops precisely so it does not
   * disturb the left track.
   */
  .spine__panel {
    /* The user-agent gives <figure> `margin: 1em 40px`. styles/surfaces.css
       zeroes it for `figure.sv-raised` and NOT for `.surface`, so it is zeroed
       here now that this panel is a `.surface`. Deleting this line puts 40px of
       inset back on the drawing and takes the rail off the panel edges. */
    margin: 0;
    padding-block: var(--blk-pad-md);
  }

  .spine {
    /* KEPT, and it is not redundant with the panel's own `position: relative`.
       `.spine__stop::before` is absolutely positioned and `.spine__stop` is
       not positioned, so this element is that pseudo-element's containing
       block. Dropping it here would silently re-resolve it against the panel
       and move it. Whether the pseudo-element's `inset` was ever written for
       this containing block is a separate question and not one this change
       answers: the job here was to move the panel treatment without moving a
       pixel. */
    position: relative;
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    /* `padding: 0`, WHICH IS ALSO A FIX. This rule used to set `padding-block`
       only, and `list-style: none` removes the marker without removing the
       user-agent's 40px `padding-inline-start`. Measured at 1440 before this
       change: the stops ran 229 to 1252 inside a panel running 188 to 1252, so
       the rule they draw with their own border-top started 40px inside the
       panel while .spine__track — which is `inset-inline: 0` on the padding
       box — ran the full width. The travelling light crossed 40px of panel with
       no rule under it. The stops and the track have agreed on the panel's own
       inline edges ever since. The same leak was in `.bs__spine` in
       BothSides.astro. */
    padding: 0;
    /*
     * ── THREE EQUAL COLUMNS ARE THE FIXED TRACK (owner decision 2, 2026-08-05)
     *
     * Equal columns at zero gap put every stop's left edge at a CONSTANT PITCH
     * of exactly one column width, and `.spine__stop` then takes the same inline
     * padding on every stop, so the numerals and the labels all begin on one
     * vertical line per column and the lines are evenly spaced. That is the
     * whole of what the S3 frame's alignment buys, and it is what retires three
     * measured defects at once:
     *
     *   the row leaned 118.7px (9.6%) left of the centred heading above it
     *   the gaps between figures opened out +43% then +70% left to right
     *   each numeral was centred on its BOX rather than on its INK, a 5.9px
     *   swing that no amount of centring the box could correct
     *
     * All three are properties of CENTRING variable-width numerals in unequal
     * optical columns. Left edges on a constant pitch have no such property:
     * column fill and ink centroid stop being able to move anything.
     */
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    /* No column gap. The rules above the stops have to meet to read as one
       continuous line; the padding inside each stop provides the separation. */
    gap: 0;
    /* ── EVERY STOP IS THE SAME HEIGHT, INCLUDING ACROSS A WRAP (O-47) ─────
     *
     * Three columns at 1440 is one band and the grid already levels it. When
     * the stops wrap, each band would otherwise be sized by its own tallest
     * label: measured at 834, back when this was four stops in two bands, the
     * top band ran 200.2px and the bottom one 225px, because "months between
     * published assessments" is the only label that takes two lines. Bands at
     * two heights put the 2px accent rules that head them at uneven intervals.
     *
     * `1fr` on the implicit rows makes every band the height of the tallest,
     * which is the whole fix and has no number in it — a fourth stop, a longer
     * label or a different breakpoint all resolve themselves. A min-height
     * would have been the same mistake styles/card-row.css records about the
     * button width floor: it equalises the pairs below it and drifts above it.
     *
     * IT IS KEPT EVEN THOUGH THIS SET NO LONGER WRAPS INTO A GRID. The layout
     * below 1024 is now ONE column rather than two, so there is no side-by-side
     * band left for it to level. It stays because it costs nothing, because it
     * is the property that makes the rule true for any STOPS length, and
     * because deleting it would quietly re-arm the defect for whoever adds a
     * fourth figure.
     *
     * WHAT IT DOES NOT FIX, STATED. A one-line label beside a two-line one
     * still leaves more air under the shorter, because the labels sit at the
     * top of their own cells and always will. The cells are identical; the copy
     * is not, and copy is the only lever left for that. */
    grid-auto-rows: 1fr;
  }

  /*
   * ── LEFT TO A FIXED TRACK, NOT CENTRED (owner decision 2, 2026-08-05) ──────
   *
   * Adopted from the Figma frame "S3 — Proportional" (node `51:36`): numerals
   * left-aligned to a fixed track on a shared baseline, with the tag above and
   * the label below aligned to the SAME track. One vertical line per stop,
   * three lines at a constant pitch, which is what `.spine` sets up above.
   *
   * THE SHARED BASELINE IS ALREADY STRUCTURAL AND IS NOT RE-ASSERTED HERE. Each
   * stop is a flex column whose first child is a one-line mono tag, and the grid
   * levels the rows, so the numerals start at the same y without any rule saying
   * so. Anything that lets a tag wrap to two lines breaks it silently.
   *
   * ── WHAT THIS REPLACED, AND WHY THE OLD REASON WAS RIGHT AND STILL LOST ───
   *
   * This block used to argue for CENTRING, on owner review of 2026-08-02: "the
   * numbers and text are not centrally aligned". That fix was real — the stop's
   * content box was asymmetrically padded and sat 14.4px left of its own column
   * centre — and centring did correct it. What centring could never correct is
   * the class of defect underneath it, because centring variable-width numerals
   * IS that class: measured at 1440, the row still leaned 118.7px (9.6%) left of
   * the centred heading above it, the gaps between figures opened out +43% then
   * +70% left to right, and every numeral sat on the centre of its BOX rather
   * than of its INK, a 5.9px swing. A left track has none of those degrees of
   * freedom. The 2026-08-02 complaint is answered better by this than by the
   * fix it asked for, which is why this is a reversal and not a regression.
   *
   * styles/surfaces.css says a card is scanned so its content centres, and prose
   * is read so it stays left. That rule is about a CARD; this is not a row of
   * cards, it is one drawing with three stops hung off a single rule, and a
   * drawing's parts align to the drawing.
   *
   * ── THE RAIL IS FULL TRACK, AND THE REFUSAL OF THE PROPORTIONAL BAR IS THE ─
   *    LOAD-BEARING PART OF THIS DECISION
   *
   * S3 draws a BAR under each numeral whose length is proportional to the
   * figure. That is refused, and the reason is not aesthetic:
   *
   *   ONLY 10% HAS A DENOMINATOR. It is a share of a total evaluation score, so
   *   a bar filling a tenth of its track states something true. £5m is a
   *   threshold and 12 is an interval; neither is a part of any whole, so there
   *   is no length that means anything. A bar drawn for them would encode
   *   nothing while LOOKING like data, on a site whose entire argument is that
   *   every figure traces to something. The Figma frame concedes the point
   *   itself: its own fourth bar is drawn at an arbitrary 30%.
   *
   * So each stop's rule fills its whole track, always, for every stop, and the
   * three meet as one continuous line across the panel in the section's existing
   * accent ramp. The line's job is to say "these three are one set, read this
   * way". It carries no magnitude and must never be given one: anything that
   * makes this border a percentage, a `width`, a `scaleX` or a gradient stop
   * derived from a value is re-adding the bar this note refuses.
   *
   * THE SPINE STILL MEETS ITSELF. The rule is `border-top` on the <li>, which
   * spans the full column whatever the padding inside it does, so the alignment
   * change moves the content and not the line.
   */
  .spine__stop {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: start;
    gap: var(--stack-1);
    /* This is the spine. Three segments, one line, full track each. */
    border-top: 2px solid var(--accent);
    /*
     * ONE TOKEN ON BOTH INLINE SIDES, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THE TRACK A TRACK.
     * The columns are equal and the gap is zero, so an identical inline padding
     * on every stop offsets every left edge by the identical amount and leaves
     * the pitch exactly one column wide. Any per-stop or asymmetric value here
     * bends the track and re-opens the +43%/+70% gap drift recorded above.
     *
     * --blk-pad, WHICH IS ALSO THE END OF A HAND-TUNED CONSTANT. This was a bare
     * `12px`, set when four columns were 266px wide and 28.8px each side left
     * less room than the numeral needed. Three columns are 354.7px at 1440, so
     * the site's default card pad fits with 286.7px of content box left over,
     * and the panel's own inline padding is 0 by design, so this is also the
     * only thing holding the first numeral off the panel's hairline.
     */
    padding: var(--blk-pad) var(--blk-pad) 0;
    text-align: start;
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* The instrument's name, in the slot the stage word used to occupy. Mono,
     letter-spaced and uppercase, so it reads as a machine label rather than as
     a sentence fragment, which is the whole reason it can be this short. */
  .spine__tag {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .spine__value {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-numeral);
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: var(--c-text);
    /*
     * `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` WAS HERE AND IS DELETED, AND THE
     * COMMENT THAT JUSTIFIED IT WAS FALSE (directive W8). It read: "Figures
     * share a width so the four do not visibly jitter against each other, which
     * is the whole reason tabular figures exist." Tabular figures only equalise
     * DIGITS WITHIN ONE STRING. They cannot make "£5m" and "10%" the same width,
     * and the measured spread across the old four was 3.93:1 with the property
     * on. The one thing it did do — equalise digits inside "12" and "10%" — pays
     * for itself only where a number CHANGES in place, and this section's other
     * standing rule is that these numbers never animate and never count up.
     * So it bought nothing and claimed to buy the section's whole alignment.
     * The alignment is bought by the fixed left track instead, which is a
     * property of the layout rather than of the font.
     */
  }

  .spine__label {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
    /* Reading measure only. `margin-inline: auto` was here to centre a 26ch
       block inside a centred column, and it is gone with the centring: on the
       left track it would push the label off the line the numeral above it
       starts on, which is the one thing this layout exists to hold. */
    max-width: 26ch;
  }

  /* THE ONLY LINK IN THE SECTION, and the only thing left where 90 words of
     source note used to be. Cyan is the interactive colour sitewide. */
  .spine__more {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) 0 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .spine__more a {
    /* 44px, WCAG 2.2 target size. A standalone action in its own row does not
       get the inline exception. */
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: var(--btn-h-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-interactive);
  }

  .spine__more a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  /* ══ M2 · THE LIGHT ON THE SPINE ══════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Figma `144:2`, M2 — "A light travels the spine. The figures never move."
   * TRIGGER IntersectionObserver, threshold 0.35.
   * CADENCE 3 beats, one per spine stop, on the shared --m-beat. It was 4, and
   *         it changed because the set became three figures on 2026-08-05, not
   *         because anything about the timing was re-opened.
   *
   * PLAYS: ONCE, ON REVEAL. IT LOOPED UNTIL 2026-08-05 AND THE OWNER STOPPED
   * IT, AND THIS FILE HAS NOW ARGUED BOTH SIDES, SO BOTH ARE RECORDED.
   *
   * It played once originally, on the argument that a highlight sweeping past a
   * reader every few seconds while they decide whether to follow the /sources
   * link is the autoplay problem. It was then made to loop with a long rest —
   * 2.4s of travel against 9.6s dark — because the owner had asked for
   * continuous play across the homepage, and the answer taken was the REST
   * rather than the refusal.
   *
   * A-104 (owner, 2026-08-05) reverses that for this section by name: statutory
   * figures "loses most of its animation". A-111 then measured what "most"
   * actually was, per section at 1440, and this one carried EIGHT perpetual
   * animations: the travel, the hue ramp, four segment flashes, and the two
   * ambient fields. Six of those eight are in this block and every one of them
   * is now a one-shot fired by the same `data-lit` reveal. The remaining two are
   * the shared `[data-field] > b` drift in styles/tokens.css, which is one
   * decision for six sections and cannot honestly be taken in this file.
   *
   * NOT ONE MILLISECOND OF THE GESTURE CHANGED WHEN THE LOOP WENT. The travel
   * was 4 beats inside a 20-beat cycle and became 4 beats flat; the segment
   * flashes were 240ms and still are. What went is the sixteen beats of dark and
   * the repeat after them, which is the whole of what a reader was objecting to.
   * The rest state is unchanged and is what the page holds afterwards: the band
   * parks at opacity 0 and every rule sits on its accent.
   *
   * THE PASS IS AS MANY BEATS AS THE SPINE HAS STOPS, WHICH IS NOW THREE. It is
   * not 1,600ms any more because 1,600ms was this section's own number and
   * agreed with nothing else on the page. See the motion grammar in
   * styles/tokens.css: a beat is one station everywhere, so this light and the
   * five-step light two sections down travel at comparable speed and differ only
   * in how far they go. The light therefore covers the same panel in three beats
   * rather than four — a station-per-beat cadence, held, rather than a fixed
   * duration that would have quietly slowed the pass by a third.
   *
   * STILL NO COUNT-UP, AND THE BOARD AGREES. Frame `144:158` records the
   * proposal being REFUSED by name: the legacy build animated these figures from
   * zero on scroll, which is the hide-then-reveal pattern that left four of nine
   * homepage sections permanently invisible when the observer did not fire. A
   * number that might not arrive is worse than a number that does not animate.
   * Nothing in this section changes a figure, a label or a tag at any point.
   *
   * LINEAR, NOT THE BOARD'S EASE CURVE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. `144:2` names
   * cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) while its own keyframe columns sit at t = 0,
   * 520, 1,040 and 1,600ms — even divisions. Those two disagree: that curve would
   * put every segment flash inside the first sixth of the pass and then crawl.
   * Even spacing is what makes each stop light as the highlight actually
   * reaches it, so the travel is linear and the delays are its thirds.
   */
  /*
   * THE TRACK EXISTS TO CLIP, AND THE CLIP IS NOT COSMETIC.
   *
   * The light is a full-width element that translates from -50% to +50%, which
   * is how the band scales with the measure instead of being a fixed 100px.
   * That means it is HALF THE MEASURE OUTSIDE ITS PARENT at each end of the
   * travel. Without this wrapper the page's scrollWidth grew by 344px at 1440
   * for the duration of the animation, which is a horizontal-overflow failure
   * that only exists while the light is moving and is therefore invisible to
   * any check that measures a settled page. Measured, not guessed.
   *
   * Every travelling light on this page needs the same treatment. The hero's
   * pulse and the integrations arrows already had it (their rails clip), and
   * FinalCta's sweep is inside a card that already sets overflow: hidden.
   */
  .spine__track {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    /* 24px tall and centred on the 2px rule, which occupies y = 0..2 of the
       spine because the stops draw it with their own border-top. The height is
       headroom for the underglow: a track clipped to 2px would cut the 12px
       pool off flat and leave a bare white line. */
    /* The rail is one panel pad below the panel top rather than on the
       section's own top edge, so the track that clips the travelling light
       moves with it. Expressed against the same token the padding uses, so the
       two cannot come apart — which is why this line moved to --blk-pad-md in
       the same edit that moved .spine__panel onto that rung, and why anything
       changing one must change the other. */
    top: calc(var(--blk-pad-md) - 11px);
    inset-inline: 0;
    height: 24px;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .spine__light {
    /* No animation and no trigger: zero pixels. The element exists so the
       stylesheet has something to move, and it is invisible until it does. */
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .spine__light {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      inset-inline: 0;
      top: 11px;
      height: 2px;
      /* WHITE CORE, HUE FROM THE SEGMENT BENEATH. `color` is what the gradient
         and the underglow both read, and it is animated through the same three
         accents the stops carry, so the light takes the colour of whatever it
         is currently over rather than having a colour of its own. */
      color: var(--c-ambient-start);
      /* --grad-beam, hue taken from `color` so the light takes the colour of
         whatever segment it is currently over. --beam-w is 6% and not the
         default 16% because this element is the full 1160px spine rather than
         one cell of a rail; 16% would make the light 370px wide. The bloom stop
         sat at half the half-width here and at 0.375 of it elsewhere, which is
         the drift the token ends. */
      /* --beam-hue REMOVED 2026-08-05: inert, for the reason recorded at
         --grad-beam in tokens.css. It asked for the stop accent and painted
         teal. The beam is achromatic now; a travelling light is decoration and
         the approved gradient rule keeps hue for what a section is about. */
      --beam-w: 6%;
      background: var(--grad-beam);
      /* THE UNDERGLOW. A soft pool that travels with the highlight and takes
         its hue from the same `color`, so it has no independent timing and
         cannot drift out of step with the thing casting it. */
      box-shadow: 0 0 12px currentColor;
      opacity: 0;
    }

    /* ONE PASS, ON REVEAL. The duration is the beats the light actually travels
       for — one per stop, so three — rather than the full twenty-beat cycle with
       sixteen beats of nothing on the end: an animation that is still "running"
       while it holds a rest state is one document.getAnimations() keeps counting,
       and A-111's whole finding is that this page's count only ever climbs. */
    [data-lit='on'] .spine__light {
      animation:
        ms-travel calc(var(--m-beat) * 3) var(--m-ease-travel) 1,
        ms-hue calc(var(--m-beat) * 3) var(--m-ease-travel) 1;
    }

    /* Each stop's rule lifts to a specular white as the highlight passes and
       falls back to its own accent. The figure, the label and the tag are not
       touched: this is light on a stroke, which is the only surface on the page
       it is safe to put light on. */
    /* 240ms, ONCE, per stop. This was 2% of the twenty-beat cycle, which is the
       same 240ms written as a fraction of a clock that no longer runs. Stated
       as the duration it is, in the same shape as the one-shot refusals in
       BothSides and ReasoningTrace, so the three read alike. */
    [data-lit='on'] .spine__stop {
      animation: ms-segment 240ms var(--m-ease-land) 1;
    }

    /* THE DELAYS ARE WHERE EACH STOP ACTUALLY IS, not round numbers. The band is
       drawn at the middle of a full-width element translating from -50% to +50%,
       so its position is exactly t/pass across the spine; three equal stops have
       their centres at 1/6, 3/6 and 5/6 of that, and the pass is three beats, so
       those are 0.5, 1.5 and 2.5 beats. The arithmetic is identical to the
       four-stop version it replaces — centre k of n at n beats is always
       (k - 0.5) beats — which is why only the fourth line was deleted and no
       number on the surviving three moved.

       THESE WERE nth-child 2 TO 5 AND ARE NOW 1 TO 3, and the reason is worth
       recording because it is the trap in counting positions rather than
       things. The track used to be the <ol>'s first child, which was invalid
       markup and also silently made every stop's index one higher than its
       ordinal. The track now sits in the .spine__panel <figure> alongside the
       list, so a stop's index is its ordinal again. Anything that moves a
       non-<li> into or out of this list must re-check these lines. */
    [data-lit='on'] .spine__stop:nth-child(1) { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 0.5); }
    [data-lit='on'] .spine__stop:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 1.5); }
    [data-lit='on'] .spine__stop:nth-child(3) { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 2.5); }
  }

  /* The band is drawn at the middle of a full-width element and the element
     translates, so the travel is one transform on the compositor and the band
     scales with the measure instead of being a fixed 100px that reads wide on a
     phone and narrow on a monitor. */
  /* THE BLOCK IS THE TRAVEL AND NOTHING ELSE. It read 0% -> 20% with sixteen
     beats of held rest state after it, because the animation ran the full
     --m-cycle and repeated. Every offset below is the same instant it always
     was, restated as a fraction of the pass: 2% of the cycle is 10% of the pass,
     18% is 90%. The dark tail is gone because the animation ends there instead
     of waiting to start again. The offsets are proportions, so shortening the
     pass from four beats to three moved none of them.

     THE LAST OFFSET IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE RESTING STATE, and that is what
     keeps this a light rather than an entrance. scripts/check-motion.js rule 2
     reads a keyframe block that starts translated and ends untranslated, or
     starts below opacity 1 and ends at 1, as an arrival that belongs in
     motion.css. This band starts off the left edge at opacity 0 and ends off
     the RIGHT edge at opacity 0, so it crosses and leaves; it never resolves to
     where the element rests. Anything that "tidies" the last offset to
     translateX(0) turns a travelling light into a reveal and fails that gate. */
  @keyframes ms-travel {
    0% {
      transform: translateX(-50%);
      opacity: 0;
    }
    10% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    90% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    100% {
      transform: translateX(50%);
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  /* The accents are a DIRECTION ramp that is already approved and already
     shipped on the stops. The light rides them rather than replacing them, and
     it must ride the SAME three the stops carry: with three stops the ramp runs
     start -> mid -> end and --c-ambient-far is not on the row, so a light that
     still ended on orchid would be painting a hue nothing under it holds.
     The ramp is spent evenly across the pass, one accent per stop. */
  @keyframes ms-hue {
    0%      { color: var(--c-ambient-start); }
    50%     { color: var(--c-ambient-mid); }
    100%    { color: var(--c-ambient-end); }
  }

  /* Border colour only, and it opens and closes on --accent, so the rule is
     byte-identical to its rest state before the flash and after it. 240ms end
     to end with the white at 45% of that, which is where 0.9% of a 12s cycle
     sat: the flash is the same length and the same shape, and only the clock it
     is written against changed. */
  @keyframes ms-segment {
    0%    { border-top-color: var(--accent); }
    45%   { border-top-color: var(--c-text); }
    100%  { border-top-color: var(--accent); }
  }

  /* ══ THE STOP ANSWERS A CURSOR ════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * This section had ZERO :hover rules. specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.6 names
   * it as one of the four sections where nothing on screen answers a pointer,
   * and puts the whole page at 2.7% of elements carrying a transition against
   * 16.2% on the dark comparator.
   *
   * WHAT A STOP DOES WHEN THE POINTER IS ON IT: the column stands up onto the
   * fourth surface step, its accent rule goes specular white, the instrument
   * tag brightens, the figure's cast deepens, and the label comes up from
   * --c-text-sub to --c-text. Five things, one event.
   *
   * THE BACKGROUND CHANGE IS THE ONE THING TO CHECK IF THIS IS EVER EDITED.
   * It is a ::before, so nothing about the stop reflows, and it sits at
   * z-index -1 inside the stop's own isolated stacking context, so it is behind
   * the tag, the figure and the label rather than over them. Both ENDPOINTS
   * clear the contrast floor with room: --c-text on --c-lit is about 19:1 and
   * --c-text-sub on --c-lit about 12:1, so every intermediate frame of the
   * transition does too, which is what the axe gate samples for. This section
   * is two below the one that has already failed that gate once.
   *
   * NOTHING TRANSLATES. A stop is a figure and a label, not a control, and
   * styles/surfaces.css is explicit that lifting text under a pointer
   * advertises an affordance that is not there.
   *
   * THE ACCENT NEVER CHANGES HUE. It goes to white and back, which is the same
   * thing the travelling light does to it once, on reveal. Teal, cyan and
   * violet stay exactly where the direction ramp put them.
   *
   * AND THIS BLOCK IS NOW MOST OF THE SECTION'S MOTION, WHICH IS THE POINT OF
   * A-104 ITEM 6 RATHER THAN A SIDE EFFECT OF IT. Once the ambient loops stop,
   * what is left is an entrance that fires once and a response that fires
   * whenever a reader asks for it. That is the trade the owner's instruction
   * buys: less motion happening AT the reader, the same amount available TO
   * them.
   */
  .spine__stop,
  .spine__tag,
  .spine__value,
  .spine__label {
    transition:
      color var(--dur) var(--ease),
      border-color var(--dur) var(--ease),
      filter var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  .spine__stop::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    z-index: -1;
    /* Reaches up over the 2px rule so the lit surface starts AT the rail rather
       than below it, and stops short of the bottom so the panel underneath
       still reads through. */
    inset: -2px 0 12%;
    /* --radius-card, NOT --radius-panel, AND THE SCALE IS THE ARGUMENT.
       tokens.css reads --radius-panel as "a panel that HOLDS cards" and
       --radius-card as "a card". This lit backing is one column INSIDE the
       panel — it is the card, and the panel is the thing it stands on — so a
       20px corner on it sat one rung above the 16px corner of the box
       containing it. Two nested blocks whose inner corner is rounder than the
       outer is the tell that a radius was picked rather than derived. */
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    /* --grad-ramp from the fourth surface step down to nothing, so the panel
       underneath still reads through. `transparent` written plainly: the old
       `color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-lit) 0%, transparent)` resolves to exactly
       the same rgba(0,0,0,0) and only looked like it meant something. */
    --ramp-from: var(--c-lit);
    --ramp-to: transparent;
      /* Written here, not var(--grad-ramp). A custom property's var()s are
         substituted where it is DECLARED, so a knob token on :root bakes in its
         fallbacks and inherits finished. Measured: --grad-ramp computes to
         `linear-gradient(180deg, #161B2F, #0E1220 100%)` on every element,
         whatever knobs it sets. It hid every blog hero photo behind an opaque
         rectangle before this was found. Colours still come from tokens. */
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--c-lit), transparent 100%);
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    .spine__stop:hover::before { opacity: 1; }

    .spine__stop:hover { border-top-color: var(--c-text); }

    .spine__stop:hover .spine__tag {
      filter: brightness(1.35);
      text-shadow: 0 0 14px color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 55%, transparent);
    }

    /* The numeral is painted through a gradient clipped to the glyph, so its
       shadow has to come off the paint rather than off the text: a
       text-shadow on a transparent fill draws nothing. Same reason
       `sv-numeral` uses a filter for its rest shadow. */
    .spine__stop:hover .spine__value {
      filter: drop-shadow(0 2px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.9))
        drop-shadow(0 0 26px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent));
    }

    .spine__stop:hover .spine__label { color: var(--c-text); }

    .spine__more a:hover { color: var(--c-text); }
  }

  /* ── THREE COLUMNS, THEN ONE. THERE IS NO TWO-COLUMN STATE ANY MORE ────────
   *
   * A column needs roughly 240px before the labels start breaking badly, which
   * is the same figure this block has always been written against. It used to
   * read "Two, then one", because four columns hit that floor at 1024. Three
   * columns do not: at 1024 they are about 325px each, comfortably clear.
   *
   * THE TWO-COLUMN STATE IS DELETED RATHER THAN CARRIED FORWARD, AND THE REASON
   * IS THE RAIL. Three items in two columns wrap 2 + 1, so the second row holds
   * one stop and its border-top draws a rule across HALF the panel and stops.
   * A rule that stops halfway is not a weaker version of one continuous line,
   * it is a broken drawing, and this section is a drawing before it is a grid.
   * One column has the opposite property: every stop's rule is full track by
   * definition, which is the same thing the desktop row says.
   *
   * THE LEFT TRACK IS WHY ONE COLUMN COSTS NOTHING HERE. Centred content in a
   * single 975px column would look stranded; left-aligned content simply
   * continues the alignment the desktop row already has, so the two states are
   * the same drawing at two widths rather than two layouts.
   *
   * THE NUMERAL OVERRIDE WENT WITH THE TWO-COLUMN STATE. It read
   * clamp(2.75rem, 8.2vw, 5.5rem) and existed because two columns were far
   * wider than four, so the figure could take more of its clamp than a 266px
   * column allowed. There is no such squeeze left to compensate for: the figure
   * now has the full measure. --t-numeral governs at every width, which is one
   * fewer place for the sizes to disagree and is the direction the owner's
   * "the figures read too big" pushed the token in the first place. */
  @media (max-width: 1023px) {
    .spine {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      row-gap: var(--stack-2);
    }

    /* THE SPINE STOPS BEING ONE LINE HERE, so the light has nothing to travel.
       Stacked rules cannot be crossed by one horizontal highlight, and a light
       that runs along the top row and ignores the rest is worse than no light.
       The per-segment flash stays: it is per-stop and needs no geometry. */
    .spine__track { display: none; }
  }

  /* The reading measure is the only thing left to drop, and it drops because a
     26ch cap inside a phone-width column just leaves a ragged right edge. */
  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    .spine__label { max-width: none; }
  }
</style>
